From bea6cb492ccab1693314c0609565e2818f53422d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:57:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: HDR of more than 8 bits through Kodi's own
 buffers where EGL has no deeper window

Video of more than 8 bits (HDR10, HLG, Dolby Vision rebuilt as HDR10) goes out at 10 bits on
nvidia-drm with the 580 driver: where its planes take buffers of 10 bits and its EGL offers no window
of them, Kodi draws into buffers that it allocates itself (COwnScanout) and commits those.

When: SetVideoOutput() for more than 8 bits, with GLES, on nvidia-drm (NV_INPUT_COLORSPACE on its
planes), when ChooseEGLConfig() finds no window of that depth. Where EGL has one (the 615 driver: half
floats; Mesa: 10 bits) nothing changes. advancedsettings.xml:
<video><hdrownbuffersalways>true</hdrownbuffersalways></video> uses Kodi's own buffers there too, with
any driver (to try this way on an RTX with the 615 driver), and <hdrownbuffers>false</hdrownbuffers>
never.

Which: the active 10-bit formats of the planes first, in their order (AB30, then XB30), then half
floats where FloatOutputUsable() allows the half-float output. 10 bits are the depth of HDR10 itself,
sent as they are with "None" as the input colour space (read in NVKMS 580 and 615: identity matrices
and output table, as for a half-float buffer; nvkms-modeset.c also hints depth 30 to headSurface only
for A2B10G10R10/X2B10G10R10), like the 10-bit window of the Mesa drivers, with half the memory of half
floats and nothing outside [0, 1] to clamp.

Before use: the buffers are made and probed (previous commit) and the driver takes one of them in a
test commit (TestFramebuffer()). A failure keeps the output of today (the window, at 8 bits) with
one line that says which step failed, and that display, mode and format are not tried again. In
use, a commit that the driver refuses is met as for a half-float output (CheckFloatOutput()): when the
refusal is about the buffer, or may be, the window's buffers are put back, for the rest of the video
(three videos in a row: then seven without), and what the refused commit carried is queued again.

How: the window of EGL stays, unswapped, and is the output again at the end of the video (8 bits),
when the window is made anew (another size) or after a failure; the plane gets back the format and
the modifier it had. Every swap of a frame in the GLES window system goes through
SwapOutputBuffers(): eglSwapBuffers(), or the frame done in Kodi's own buffer (flushed; finished
when there is no fence for the commit). The buffer of the next frame is bound after each flip and in
BeginRender(), and is the framebuffer that stands for the window (OutputFramebuffer()). The fencing,
the commits and the queue are those of the window: Kodi's commit takes the front buffer through
CGBMSurface::LockFrontBuffer(). The buffer age is 0 with them, and for the first frames of the window
after them. The buffers that went are destroyed once Kodi's queue has given them back and two flips
of others have come (30 flips at most). GetOutputBitDepth(), HasFloatOutput() and OutputBufferDeep()
say what these buffers are, so that the link depth (LinkDepth.h), the captures and native Dolby
Vision (which starts only at 8 bits) see them.

In kodi.log: "HDR output: video of more than 8 bits goes out at 10 bits through 3 buffers of AB30
(modifier ...) that Kodi allocates itself ...", and then the depth of the link, "HDR output: HDR10
goes out at 10 bits in ..., as the driver says (NV_OUTPUT_BPC)".

Only hardware shows: the allocation for scanout, the import, the commits, the flips and what NVKMS
sends. Not seen on any GPU.
